  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 7,
    "lesson_title": "Amplitude Scaling by $|H(j\\omega)|$",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute the output sinusoid's amplitude by multiplying the input amplitude by $|H(j\\omega)|$ at the input frequency for sinusoidal steady-state passage through an LTI system.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Amplitude Scaling by $|H(j\\omega)|$",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Amplitude Scaling by $|H(j\\omega)|$",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In sinusoidal steady state through an LTI system, the output sinusoid keeps the same angular frequency $\\omega_0$. The change to watch is the amplitude.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the base case $x(t)=A\\cos(\\omega_0 t)$, track the input amplitude $A_{\\text{in}}$ and the gain magnitude $|H(j\\omega_0)|$ at that same frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "A_{\\text{out}}=A_{\\text{in}}\\,|H(j\\omega_0)|",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the output amplitude is the input amplitude multiplied by the magnitude of the frequency response at $\\omega_0$. The output frequency stays fixed at $\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=A\\cos(\\omega_0 t)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;A_{\\text{out}}=A\\,|H(j\\omega_0)|",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read the size of $|H(j\\omega_0)|$ this way: values less than $1$ mean attenuation, values equal to $1$ mean unity gain, and values greater than $1$ mean amplification.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|H(j\\omega_0)|<1,\\quad |H(j\\omega_0)|=1,\\quad |H(j\\omega_0)|>1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Amplitude Scaling by $|H(j\\omega)|$",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Scaling an Amplitude Using a Given $|H(j\\omega_0)|$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A sinusoid with amplitude $A_{\\text{in}}=8$ enters an LTI system whose magnitude at the input frequency is $|H(j\\omega_0)|=0.5$. Find the output amplitude.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the cleanest numerical case: the gain magnitude is already given, so the only job is to scale the input amplitude.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read off $A_{\\text{in}}=8$ and $|H(j\\omega_0)|=0.5$ from the prompt. Then apply the amplitude rule by multiplying those two numbers.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "A_{\\text{out}}=A_{\\text{in}}\\,|H(j\\omega_0)|=8(0.5)=4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the output amplitude is $4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Amplitude Scaling by $|H(j\\omega)|$",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Evaluating $|H(j\\omega)|$ at the Input Frequency",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A sinusoid $x(t)=5\\cos(4t)$ enters an LTI system with $H(j\\omega)=\\frac{10}{3+j\\omega}$. Find the output amplitude.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This example adds one new wrinkle: the gain factor is not given directly, so first evaluate the frequency response at the input frequency $\\omega_0=4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read the input amplitude as $A_{\\text{in}}=5$. Then substitute $\\omega_0=4$ into $H(j\\omega)$ and take the magnitude.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|H(j4)|=\\left|\\frac{10}{3+j4}\\right|=\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{3^2+4^2}}=\\frac{10}{5}=2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now apply the amplitude rule with that magnitude: multiply the input amplitude by $2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "A_{\\text{out}}=A_{\\text{in}}\\,|H(j\\omega_0)|=5(2)=10",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the output amplitude is $10$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Amplitude Scaling by $|H(j\\omega)|$",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Computing $|H(j\\omega_0)|$ from a Complex Frequency Response Value",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For an input amplitude $5$, the system response at the input frequency is $H(j\\omega_0)=3-4j$. Find the output amplitude.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This example keeps the same amplitude rule, but the frequency-response value is given in rectangular form. The first job is to extract its magnitude.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read the input amplitude as $A_{\\text{in}}=5$ and recognize that the system response at the same input frequency is $H(j\\omega_0)=3-4j$. For a complex number $a+bj$, use $|a+bj|=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|H(j\\omega_0)|=|3-4j|=\\sqrt{3^2+(-4)^2}=5",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now use that magnitude as the amplitude gain and multiply it by the input amplitude.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "A_{\\text{out}}=A_{\\text{in}}\\,|H(j\\omega_0)|=5(5)=25",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the output amplitude is $25$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Amplitude Scaling by $|H(j\\omega)|$",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Identifying Attenuation, Unity Gain, and Amplification from $|H(j\\omega_0)|$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A system has $|H(j\\omega_0)|=0.4$ at the input frequency. Decide whether the output amplitude is attenuated, unchanged, or amplified.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This example keeps the same amplitude rule, but the new job is to classify the gain factor by comparing it with $1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read the gain factor as $|H(j\\omega_0)|=0.4$. Since $0.4<1$, the output amplitude is smaller than the input amplitude.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|H(j\\omega_0)|<1 \\Rightarrow \\text{attenuation},\\quad |H(j\\omega_0)|=1 \\Rightarrow \\text{unity gain},\\quad |H(j\\omega_0)|>1 \\Rightarrow \\text{amplification}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So this system produces attenuation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
